1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,130
In 1956, IBM shipped the first hard drive.

2
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:06,850
It held about five megabytes.

3
00:00:07,709 --> 00:00:11,049
The machine was taller than the
person operating it, and with

4
00:00:11,129 --> 00:00:14,519
the compressor that kept it
running, it weighed more than a ton.

5
00:00:15,293 --> 00:00:18,343
Five megabytes is one
photograph from a modern phone.

6
00:00:19,353 --> 00:00:23,823
Today you can hold a drive in one hand
that stores nearly ten million times more.

7
00:00:24,521 --> 00:00:26,151
So what happened in between?

8
00:00:27,556 --> 00:00:31,146
The obvious answer is that we got
better at building small machines.

9
00:00:32,016 --> 00:00:33,486
That answer isn't wrong.

10
00:00:34,076 --> 00:00:35,566
It's just the boring half.

11
00:00:36,445 --> 00:00:39,335
Because the machine got about
a thousand times lighter.

12
00:00:40,145 --> 00:00:42,915
The storage got about
a billion times denser.

13
00:00:43,562 --> 00:00:45,292
A thousand against a billion.

14
00:00:45,862 --> 00:00:49,212
Everything interesting about
storage is hiding in that gap.

15
00:00:51,902 --> 00:00:54,222
To see where the billion
came from, you have to

16
00:00:54,302 --> 00:00:56,952
start with what a hard
drive was actually for.

17
00:00:57,673 --> 00:01:01,843
Before it, computers kept their data
on punched cards and on magnetic tape.

18
00:01:02,293 --> 00:01:05,283
Tape works. But tape is a queue.

19
00:01:06,285 --> 00:01:10,735
If the thing you want sits at the end,
you travel through everything before it.

20
00:01:11,325 --> 00:01:15,095
A machine could spend its afternoon
winding backwards and forwards.

21
00:01:15,983 --> 00:01:18,053
The new machine could go straight to it.

22
00:01:18,643 --> 00:01:23,333
That was the whole point of it, and it
is what the name says. Random access.

23
00:01:24,339 --> 00:01:29,209
Fifty metal disks, sixty centimetres
across, spinning twenty times a second.

24
00:01:30,219 --> 00:01:33,729
An arm moved between them and put a
magnetic head over the right track.

25
00:01:34,485 --> 00:01:37,095
It could find any piece
of data in under a second.

26
00:01:37,985 --> 00:01:41,275
In 1956, that bordered on magic.

27
00:01:42,555 --> 00:01:45,125
And it came with a problem
that has never gone away.

28
00:01:45,895 --> 00:01:47,445
The head must not touch the disk.

29
00:01:48,519 --> 00:01:51,749
Touch it, and you scrape away
the data and the head together.

30
00:01:52,539 --> 00:01:55,669
But it has to stay near enough
to feel one magnetic patch.

31
00:01:56,575 --> 00:01:59,745
IBM's answer was to float
it on compressed air.

32
00:02:00,695 --> 00:02:03,465
The head hovered twenty
micrometres above the surface.

33
00:02:04,374 --> 00:02:06,774
Which is why the machine
needed its own air compressor.

34
00:02:06,854 --> 00:02:12,004
Two hundred kilograms of that ton
was a pump, blowing air under a head.

35
00:02:14,464 --> 00:02:17,814
And that five megabytes needs a
footnote, because it wasn't five.

36
00:02:18,184 --> 00:02:21,514
It was five million
characters of six bits each.

37
00:02:22,306 --> 00:02:25,176
In today's money, three and
three quarter megabytes.

38
00:02:26,693 --> 00:02:30,223
But here is the number that
matters, and nobody ever quotes it.

39
00:02:32,033 --> 00:02:34,953
On that disk, one square
centimetre held three hundred bits.

40
00:02:35,033 --> 00:02:37,462
Three hundred.

41
00:02:37,912 --> 00:02:42,482
A modern drive puts nearly three hundred
billion into the same square centimetre.

42
00:02:43,292 --> 00:02:45,172
That is where the billion came from.

43
00:02:45,252 --> 00:02:48,282
Not from the box. From the bit.

44
00:02:50,992 --> 00:02:55,122
The box did shrink, of course, and the
first thing to go was that compressor.

45
00:02:56,187 --> 00:03:00,707
In 1961, IBM shaped the
head like a tiny wing, so

46
00:03:00,787 --> 00:03:03,497
that the spinning disk made
its own cushion of air.

47
00:03:04,219 --> 00:03:05,869
No pump. No plumbing.

48
00:03:05,979 --> 00:03:08,739
And the head could now
fly three times nearer the

49
00:03:08,819 --> 00:03:11,609
surface, which bought
thirteen times the density.

50
00:03:12,509 --> 00:03:15,919
In 1973 came a drive nicknamed Winchester.

51
00:03:17,029 --> 00:03:18,789
It sealed the disks and the heads into one

52
00:03:18,869 --> 00:03:21,559
clean assembly, so that
nothing could get in.

53
00:03:24,059 --> 00:03:26,489
What made storage grow was never the box.

54
00:03:27,339 --> 00:03:30,949
It was making the bit smaller.
So what is a bit, physically?

55
00:03:31,903 --> 00:03:35,353
The disk is coated with a
magnetic film made of tiny grains.

56
00:03:36,063 --> 00:03:40,173
A bit is a patch of those
grains, magnetised the same way.

57
00:03:40,875 --> 00:03:45,685
Point them one way for a zero, and the
other way for a one. Want more capacity?

58
00:03:46,755 --> 00:03:48,865
Then use fewer grains for each bit.

59
00:03:49,679 --> 00:03:54,969
Every generation did exactly that.
Smaller grains. Narrower tracks.

60
00:03:55,119 --> 00:03:58,509
Arms that could aim more
precisely. But there is a price.

61
00:03:59,422 --> 00:04:01,202
A smaller patch makes a weaker signal.

62
00:04:01,282 --> 00:04:05,472
Halve the patch, and the head
has half as much to listen to.

63
00:04:06,082 --> 00:04:08,432
By the late eighties, it was getting thin.

64
00:04:10,162 --> 00:04:13,792
Then physics handed the industry
something it had not asked for.

65
00:04:14,461 --> 00:04:17,761
In 1988, Albert Fert and Peter Grünberg

66
00:04:17,841 --> 00:04:20,431
independently found the
same strange effect.

67
00:04:21,298 --> 00:04:24,928
Stack thin magnetic layers with
a sliver of metal between them,

68
00:04:25,218 --> 00:04:28,848
and the resistance of the stack
depends on how the layers line up.

69
00:04:29,501 --> 00:04:31,231
A small magnetic field.

70
00:04:31,861 --> 00:04:35,531
A large change in resistance. They
called it giant magnetoresistance.

71
00:04:37,579 --> 00:04:38,909
Which is a read head.

72
00:04:39,659 --> 00:04:43,729
A magnetic patch goes past the sensor,
and the sensor's resistance jumps.

73
00:04:44,939 --> 00:04:46,709
Small signals stop being a problem.

74
00:04:47,514 --> 00:04:50,704
IBM shipped the first one in 1997.

75
00:04:51,674 --> 00:04:54,704
The Nobel Prize for the
discovery came in 2007.

76
00:04:56,081 --> 00:05:00,151
By then the effect had been sitting
inside ordinary computers for a decade.

77
00:05:02,421 --> 00:05:04,041
And the numbers it unlocked are absurd.

78
00:05:04,121 --> 00:05:08,591
Three hundred bits per
square centimetre in 1956.

79
00:05:09,181 --> 00:05:11,171
Nearly three hundred billion now.

80
00:05:11,885 --> 00:05:15,475
Roughly a billion times more data
on the same piece of surface.

81
00:05:16,305 --> 00:05:19,135
That is how five megabytes
became terabytes.

82
00:05:22,225 --> 00:05:23,845
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

83
00:05:23,925 --> 00:05:26,795
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

84
00:05:27,705 --> 00:05:29,135
It really helps the channel.

85
00:05:30,517 --> 00:05:32,587
Except that isn't the end of the story.

86
00:05:33,417 --> 00:05:35,627
It's the point where
the story hits a wall.

87
00:05:36,461 --> 00:05:39,331
Because you cannot keep
taking grains away from a bit.

88
00:05:40,241 --> 00:05:43,611
A grain holds its direction
because flipping it costs energy.

89
00:05:44,473 --> 00:05:46,603
Shrink the grain, and that cost falls.

90
00:05:46,793 --> 00:05:49,403
And a disk is not a quiet place.

91
00:05:50,033 --> 00:05:52,943
Room temperature is atoms
shoving each other about.

92
00:05:53,832 --> 00:05:56,032
Make the grain small
enough, and an ordinary

93
00:05:56,112 --> 00:05:58,642
thermal nudge is enough to turn it over.

94
00:05:59,347 --> 00:06:00,927
The bit flips. On its own.

95
00:06:01,007 --> 00:06:02,417
Nobody wrote anything.

96
00:06:04,925 --> 00:06:07,815
Physicists call it the
superparamagnetic limit.

97
00:06:08,445 --> 00:06:10,995
Engineers had a blunter way to put it.

98
00:06:11,545 --> 00:06:13,055
They were running out of atoms.

99
00:06:14,071 --> 00:06:17,531
Around the turn of the century,
the old way of recording ran into

100
00:06:17,611 --> 00:06:21,881
it, somewhere near fifteen billion
bits per square centimetre.

101
00:06:22,465 --> 00:06:25,595
For the first time in forty years,
the curve was about to stop.

102
00:06:26,085 --> 00:06:28,575
So the industry cheated. Twice.

103
00:06:30,925 --> 00:06:32,805
The first cheat was to notice that the

104
00:06:32,885 --> 00:06:35,675
disk has a dimension
nobody had been using.

105
00:06:36,461 --> 00:06:39,041
Until then, each bit lay
flat, stretched along

106
00:06:39,121 --> 00:06:41,791
its track, like a word printed on a page.

107
00:06:42,561 --> 00:06:45,091
Flat things get thin when you shrink them.

108
00:06:45,775 --> 00:06:47,915
So the magnetisation was
turned through ninety

109
00:06:47,995 --> 00:06:51,185
degrees, to point down
into the disk instead.

110
00:06:51,994 --> 00:06:54,224
When there was no more
room on the surface,

111
00:06:54,374 --> 00:06:56,364
we started standing the bits upright.

112
00:06:57,317 --> 00:06:59,947
A standing bit can be
narrow and still be deep,

113
00:07:00,237 --> 00:07:02,827
so it keeps enough material
to hold its direction.

114
00:07:03,990 --> 00:07:06,940
Drives started shipping that way in 2005.

115
00:07:07,750 --> 00:07:10,480
Every hard drive you have
bought since works like this.

116
00:07:12,710 --> 00:07:14,420
The second cheat is stranger.

117
00:07:15,170 --> 00:07:18,120
If the material is too stable
to write, heat it first.

118
00:07:19,037 --> 00:07:20,787
So they put a laser in the head.

119
00:07:23,147 --> 00:07:25,379
It heats one spot on
the disk to about four

120
00:07:25,459 --> 00:07:28,757
hundred and fifty degrees
for a single nanosecond.

121
00:07:29,537 --> 00:07:31,767
Hot, the spot is soft enough to write.

122
00:07:32,637 --> 00:07:36,807
A nanosecond later it has cooled, and
the bit is frozen where it was put.

123
00:07:37,613 --> 00:07:40,433
There is a laser inside
the hard drive, melting the

124
00:07:40,513 --> 00:07:43,403
problem out of the way a few
hundred degrees at a time.

125
00:07:44,133 --> 00:07:47,683
This is not a laboratory. You can buy it.

126
00:07:50,223 --> 00:07:53,233
Meanwhile, somebody else was
asking a different question.

127
00:07:54,123 --> 00:07:56,193
What if we take the disk out entirely?

128
00:07:57,484 --> 00:08:01,404
No platter. No arm. No head.
Nothing that has to fly.

129
00:08:01,484 --> 00:08:02,854
Nothing that has to spin.

130
00:08:03,662 --> 00:08:05,742
Flash memory stores a
bit as trapped electric

131
00:08:05,822 --> 00:08:09,892
charge, sealed behind an
insulator inside a cell.

132
00:08:10,627 --> 00:08:14,177
Put charge in, and the cell needs
a higher voltage to switch on.

133
00:08:15,007 --> 00:08:17,497
Measure that voltage, and
you know what was stored.

134
00:08:18,341 --> 00:08:20,831
Nothing moves, so nothing has to be found.

135
00:08:21,461 --> 00:08:23,151
Every cell is equally near.

136
00:08:23,961 --> 00:08:27,471
The seek time the RAMAC was built
around simply stops existing.

137
00:08:29,481 --> 00:08:33,641
And flash ran the same race.
Smaller cells. More of them.

138
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:36,051
And it hit the same kind of wall.

139
00:08:36,835 --> 00:08:39,725
Because the charge in a cell
is not really a quantity.

140
00:08:40,595 --> 00:08:42,005
It is a number of electrons.

141
00:08:43,013 --> 00:08:46,453
Shrink the cell, and that
number falls, until a handful of

142
00:08:46,533 --> 00:08:49,983
electrons leaking away is the
difference between a one and a zero.

143
00:08:50,725 --> 00:08:52,925
And it got worse, because the industry had

144
00:08:53,005 --> 00:08:55,715
started storing more than
one bit in each cell.

145
00:08:56,431 --> 00:08:59,411
Four bits means sixteen different
charge levels inside one

146
00:08:59,491 --> 00:09:03,401
cell, and the gaps between them
are a few hundred millivolts.

147
00:09:04,015 --> 00:09:06,265
Smaller cells make those gaps smaller.

148
00:09:07,175 --> 00:09:09,475
So the same wall arrived,
in a technology with

149
00:09:09,555 --> 00:09:12,225
no moving parts, for
entirely different reasons.

150
00:09:14,885 --> 00:09:17,335
And in 2013 the answer arrived.

151
00:09:18,025 --> 00:09:19,435
It was the same answer.

152
00:09:20,231 --> 00:09:22,651
Samsung stopped making
the cells smaller and

153
00:09:22,731 --> 00:09:24,611
started stacking them
on top of each other.

154
00:09:24,691 --> 00:09:28,081
Twenty-four layers, in the first one.

155
00:09:28,756 --> 00:09:32,466
Today the count is in the hundreds,
and the roadmaps talk about a thousand.

156
00:09:33,276 --> 00:09:36,666
The chip did not get wider. It got taller.

157
00:09:38,846 --> 00:09:41,376
Put the two technologies side by side.

158
00:09:42,066 --> 00:09:44,776
The bit cannot get any
smaller, so stand it upright.

159
00:09:45,786 --> 00:09:48,536
The cell cannot get any
smaller, so stack the cells.

160
00:09:49,485 --> 00:09:54,175
Different materials. Different physics.
Different companies. The same move.

161
00:09:57,175 --> 00:10:00,625
And the old technology
did not die. It moved.

162
00:10:01,495 --> 00:10:04,595
More than nine tenths of the
storage in cloud data centres is

163
00:10:04,675 --> 00:10:08,345
still spinning disks, because
per byte it is still far cheaper.

164
00:10:09,179 --> 00:10:13,129
So the seventy-year story of storage is
not really a story about miniaturisation.

165
00:10:15,979 --> 00:10:20,019
We shrank things. We reached the
floor of what matter will put up with.

166
00:10:20,099 --> 00:10:21,729
And then we built upwards.

167
00:10:24,219 --> 00:10:27,099
Five million characters
once needed fifty disks,

168
00:10:27,239 --> 00:10:30,369
a motor, a compressor, and
a room to keep them in.

169
00:10:31,035 --> 00:10:33,795
The same data now sits
in a patch of film you

170
00:10:33,875 --> 00:10:36,325
could not see without
an electron microscope.

171
00:10:37,147 --> 00:10:39,317
What got smaller was never the machine.

172
00:10:40,247 --> 00:10:43,637
It was the amount of matter we need
in order to remember one thing.

173
00:10:44,370 --> 00:10:46,690
And every time physics
told us that was as small

174
00:10:46,770 --> 00:10:50,300
as it goes, we found another
direction to build in.
